"""One-off (Nov 2026): rebuild articles whose cards had drifted from Calabro's posts. Kept for reference; distractors were tuned afterwards."""
from _lib import *

write("mod1-tasks.json", "The Top Task List: task analysis in three steps, so every study starts from what users really do.",
 "A full task analysis exposes every decision point in a workflow, but it is slow. Calabro's shortcut is a **Top Task List**: a short, prioritised list of what users are trying to do, built with stakeholders in one working session.",
 [("Step 1: The most common tasks", "A small number of workflows account for most user activity (the 80/20 rule). Find them with **real data**: product analytics, telemetry, click tracking or logs.\n\nDon't guess, and don't ask users to guess either.", None,
   q("You're building a Top Task List. What is the best source for step 1?", ["Product analytics and usage logs", "A stakeholder brainstorm of key features", "A survey asking users what they do most", "The feature list from the product roadmap"], "Step 1 needs real behaviour data. Guessing, or asking users to guess, is exactly what the method avoids.")),
  ("Step 2: The critical or risky tasks", "Some tasks are rare but carry real weight: compliance steps, deadlines, anything essential to the system working.\n\nThe test: if something going wrong here would be serious, the task belongs on the list even if it happens once a year.", None,
   q("A task happens twice a year, but an error means a failed compliance audit. Does it belong on the Top Task List?", ["Yes, as a critical or risky task", "No, it is too rare to matter", "Only if analytics show growing usage", "Only after the common tasks are tested"], "Step 2 adds tasks that are infrequent but high-stakes.")),
  ("Step 3: Dedupe and check coverage", "Merge workflows that overlap. When two tasks are alike, keep the one that **touches more screens, UI elements or interaction points**.\n\nYou don't need a full task-analysis diagram. The list then feeds every other method: usability tests, walkthroughs and reviews take their scenarios from it.", cmp("Without a list", ["Scenarios invented from assumptions", "Trying to test everything", "Studies that don't connect"], "With a Top Task List", ["Scenarios come from real tasks", "Focus on what matters most", "Every method shares one basis"]),
   q("Two tasks on your list are nearly the same. Which one do you keep?", ["The one that touches more of the system", "The one stakeholders mention more often", "The one that is quicker to test", "Both, to be safe about coverage"], "Keep the best-representative task: the one covering more screens, elements or interaction points."))],
 [q("What is a Top Task List?", ["A short, prioritised list of what users are trying to do", "A backlog of usability issues ranked by severity", "A script of click-by-click test instructions", "A list of features ranked by stakeholder votes"], "It is a simplified task analysis: what users try to do, prioritised."),
  q("Why did Calabro create this method instead of always running a full hierarchical task analysis?", ["A full analysis is slow and needs lots of field time", "Hierarchical task analysis is no longer valid", "Stakeholders refuse to read diagrams", "Analytics tools made observation unnecessary"], "He keeps the spirit of formal task analysis but trims it, in the tradition of Nielsen's discount usability."),
  q("What are the three steps, in order?", ["Most common tasks → critical tasks → dedupe and check coverage", "Critical tasks → dedupe → most common tasks", "User goals → realistic scenario → neutral wording", "Recruit users → observe → build a diagram"], "Common first, then critical or risky, then deduplicate."),
  q("Who usually takes part in building the list?", ["The primary stakeholders, in a short working session", "Only the research team, working alone", "A panel of at least twenty end users", "Engineering leads, during sprint planning"], "Calabro walks the primary stakeholders through the three steps in a short session."),
  q("What does the finished list change about later research?", ["Studies draw their scenarios from real tasks", "Usability tests are no longer needed", "Research can skip recruiting real users", "Every feature gets tested equally often"], "The list focuses research; he calls it a multiplier for every other method.")])

write("mod1-usability-vs-concept.json", "A usability test observes behaviour; a concept test collects opinions. How to tell which one you ran.",
 "Moderated usability testing comes from behavioural science: give real users a real goal and watch what they do. Many teams say “we did a usability test” when they actually asked people what they thought.",
 [("Behaviour, not opinion", "A usability test shows **what real users do** when faced with a real goal. That is still the gold standard.\n\nA concept test asks for reactions: “What do you think this button does?” “Would you use something like this?”\n\n“Real intuitiveness is not a feeling; it is a measurable outcome.”", cmp("Concept test", ["“Would you use this?”", "“How would you imagine it working?”", "You collect quotes"], "Usability test", ["A goal, not instructions", "You observe actions", "Success is measured"]),
   q("In a session you asked “Would you use something like this?” and wrote down the answers. What did you run?", ["A concept test", "A usability test", "A cognitive walkthrough", "A heuristic evaluation"], "Collecting opinions about a design is concept testing, whatever the meeting was called.")),
  ("Write tasks as intent", "Bad instructions describe the interface:\n❌ “Click the download button.”\n❌ “Find the profile page and update your settings.”\n\nGood instructions state the intent:\n✅ “You want to keep a copy of this for your records.”\n✅ “You need to change something about how the system notifies you.”", None,
   q("Which task is written as intent?", ["You want to keep a copy of this for your records.", "Click the download button in the toolbar.", "Open the File menu and choose Export as PDF.", "Find the profile page and update your settings."], "It gives a goal and leaves the route open, so you learn whether the interface supports it.")),
  ("When concept testing fits", "Concept testing has a place: **early-stage ideas**, when you need to know if the general direction makes sense.\n\nBut most of the time teams use it where a usability test would give much better data: when there is something users could interact with.", None,
   q("When is a concept test the reasonable choice?", ["Early ideas, to check the general direction", "Validating a redesign before launch", "Finding friction in an existing checkout", "Measuring whether a flow is intuitive"], "Once users can interact with something, test behaviour instead."))],
 [q("Where does moderated usability testing come from?", ["Behavioural science", "Agile product rituals", "Marketing focus groups", "Visual design critique"], "It was built by researchers such as Gould, Lewis, Nielsen and Virzi who studied what people did, not what they said."),
  q("Which is a sign you ran a concept test, not a usability test?", ["You gathered quotes instead of observing actions", "Participants failed several of the tasks", "The prototype was built in low fidelity", "A moderator was present in the session"], "Other signs: asking how they'd imagine it working, or testing a design nobody interacts with."),
  q("What should you do before writing usability test tasks?", ["Work out what people are actually trying to do", "Decide which screens look most finished", "Ask stakeholders which features to show off", "Write the report template for the findings"], "Start with a task analysis, not the UI."),
  q("What is wrong with “Find the profile page and update your settings”?", ["It describes the interface, not the user's intent", "It is too long for participants to remember", "It tests two different pages at once", "It doesn't include a time limit"], "It hands the route to the participant, so you only test instruction-following."),
  q("How does Calabro define real intuitiveness?", ["A measurable outcome", "A feeling users report", "A sign of clean visual design", "Something experts can judge by eye"], "“Real intuitiveness is not a feeling; it is a measurable outcome.”")])

write("mod2-cognitive-walkthrough.json", "Four questions at every step, and how to stop a walkthrough turning into a demo.",
 "A cognitive walkthrough steps through a task as if you were a brand-new user. The goal is not to show how the interface works but to find where a newcomer would stall.",
 [("The four questions", "At every step, ask:\n\n**1.** Will the user know what they are trying to do?\n**2.** Will they see the control that lets them do it?\n**3.** Will they understand that this control does what they need?\n**4.** After acting, will they get feedback that it worked?", None,
   q("A user clicks Save and nothing visibly changes. Which question does this step fail?", ["Will they get feedback that it worked?", "Will they know what they are trying to do?", "Will they see the control for it?", "Will they understand what the control does?"], "The action was found and understood; the system didn't confirm it.")),
  ("Where confirmation bias creeps in", "Many teams think they ran a walkthrough when they only confirmed what they knew. Three predictable ways:\n\n• Assuming users know what the team knows\n• Skipping messy starting points: real users arrive from deep links, emails and alerts\n• Narrating steps aloud and filling in gaps without noticing", cmp("Guided demo", ["Starts at the home page", "One person narrates", "Everyone nods"], "Real walkthrough", ["Realistic entry points", "Reviewers work independently", "Disagreements recorded"]),
   q("Your team starts every walkthrough from the home page. What is the risk?", ["Real users often start from deep links or emails", "The home page takes too long to review", "Reviewers will disagree about the first step", "The four questions don't apply to home pages"], "Skipping messy starting points is one of the three ways bias creeps in.")),
  ("Six steps", "**1.** Define a specific user goal\n**2.** Select realistic entry points\n**3.** Break the workflow into atomic steps\n**4.** Assign independent reviewers\n**5.** Compare results and document disagreements\n**6.** Validate risky steps with users\n\nTwo extra safeguards: bring in a naive reviewer, and rotate facilitators.", None,
   q("Why should reviewers fill in the grid independently before comparing?", ["Disagreements between them reveal the risky steps", "It makes the session shorter for everyone", "It removes the need to test with users", "Each reviewer can then cover a different task"], "Step 5 is comparing results and documenting disagreements; those are the findings."))],
 [q("What is the goal of a cognitive walkthrough?", ["Find where a new user would stall", "Show stakeholders how the flow works", "Measure time on task precisely", "Collect opinions about the design"], "It is done as if you were a brand-new user."),
  q("Which is one of the four questions?", ["Will they see the control that lets them do it?", "Would they recommend this to a colleague?", "Is the visual design consistent here?", "How long does this step take on average?"], "Know the goal, see the control, understand it, get feedback."),
  q("A team believes “Finalize” is obviously the right label because they use the word daily. Which bias pattern is this?", ["Assuming users know what the team knows", "Skipping messy starting points", "Rotating facilitators too often", "Validating risky steps with users"], "Team vocabulary is not user vocabulary."),
  q("What is the last of the six steps?", ["Validate risky steps with users", "Define a specific user goal", "Assign independent reviewers", "Break the workflow into atomic steps"], "The walkthrough flags risk; users confirm it."),
  q("Which safeguard does Calabro suggest against confirmation bias?", ["Bring in a naive reviewer", "Let the designer narrate the flow", "Review only the happy path", "Agree on answers before the session"], "A naive reviewer and rotating facilitators both help.")])

write("mod2-fake-ai.json", "What a synthetic “proxy” user could never tell you, and why simulated research spreads.",
 "Synthetic or “proxy” users let teams simulate research without doing any. The output looks enough like insight to pass. Calabro's test is practical: what would a real person tell you that a model never could?",
 [("Ten things a proxy user never says", "Real sessions produce moments like:\n\n“I gave up halfway through because I got frustrated.”\n“I don't know what that button does, so I avoid it.”\n“I use this workaround every time because your system is too slow.”\n“I didn't even notice that feature until you pointed it out.”\n“I thought it worked. Then I realized it didn't.”\n\nThese are the unknown unknowns that make or break a project.", None,
   q("Which statement could only come from a real user?", ["“I use this workaround every time.”", "“Users generally prefer simple flows.”", "“This feature would likely be valuable.”", "“The layout appears clear and modern.”"], "A workaround is lived behaviour. The others are plausible generalities a model can produce.")),
  ("Things people hide", "Also on his list:\n\n“I lied in your survey because I thought it would help me finish faster.”\n“I feel stupid asking this, but…”\n“I clicked that by mistake, but I didn't want to say anything.”\n“I had to ask a coworker how to do it.”\n“Honestly, I don't trust your product to do this right.”", cmp("Proxy user", ["Plausible answer", "No frustration", "No workaround"], "Real user", ["Gives up", "Hides mistakes", "Asks a coworker"]),
   q("A participant says “I clicked that by mistake, but I didn't want to say anything.” What does this show?", ["Real sessions surface behaviour people hide", "The participant was poorly recruited", "The moderator asked a leading question", "The prototype needs higher fidelity"], "Embarrassment and hidden errors only appear with real people.")),
  ("Scaling the decay", "Calabro's biggest worry is not that proxy users give PMs a shortcut to their own opinions. It is that **whole teams can build fast on simulated research**.\n\nHe traces it to years of speed over quality: trading synthesis for sentiment and pushing researchers out of early stages.", None,
   q("What does Calabro see as the biggest threat of proxy users?", ["Teams scale up work built on simulated research", "AI tools are too expensive for small teams", "Participants will lose paid research work", "PMs will stop attending research sessions"], "They scale the decay of research across entire teams."))],
 [q("What is a “proxy user” in this article?", ["An AI stand-in for real research participants", "A colleague who role-plays a customer", "A power user who represents a segment", "A persona built from interview data"], "It is the synthetic-user idea: talking to a chatbot instead of people."),
  q("Why does synthetic output get accepted?", ["It looks enough like insight to pass", "It is statistically more reliable", "It always matches analytics data", "Researchers usually recommend it"], "The output is coherent and plausible, which is the danger."),
  q("Which is on Calabro's list of things a proxy user never says?", ["“I had to ask a coworker how to do it.”", "“I would rate this four out of five.”", "“The onboarding seems well structured.”", "“I prefer the second design option.”"], "Asking a coworker is a real workaround in a real setting."),
  q("What do the ten statements have in common?", ["They uncover unknown unknowns", "They are all complaints about speed", "They come from expert users only", "They can be predicted in advance"], "They are the parts of a session that lead to real learning."),
  q("Where does Calabro say the trend comes from?", ["Years of pushing for speed over quality", "A shortage of trained UX researchers", "Stricter privacy rules on recruiting", "Users refusing to join studies"], "PMs didn't set out to break research, but speed-first habits led here.")])

write("mod2-cognitive-biases.json", "20 biases in three groups, each with an antidote. Naming the bias is what slows a bad decision.",
 "Calabro's guide started as a reference for one UX team. It gives each of 20 biases a definition, an example and an antidote, so a team shares vocabulary. Read the group that matches where you are in the work.",
 [("Group 1: Study design (10)", "These creep in when you design a study or write a task.\n\n**Framing effect:** how you ask shapes the answer.\n**Serial-position effect:** first and last list items seem more valuable.\n**Self-reporting bias:** reported values differ from actual ones.\n**Base-rate neglect:** misreading what small samples do to percentages.\n**Power-user fallacy:** assuming frequent users behave drastically differently.", None,
   q("You open a task with “Others found this difficult, so take your time.” Which bias did you introduce?", ["Framing effect", "Recall bias", "Sunk cost fallacy", "False consensus effect"], "The set-up shapes the response. Write neutral task introductions.")),
  ("Group 2: Interpretation (7)", "These come from inside our own heads during synthesis.\n\n**Confirmation bias:** only looking for evidence that supports your hypothesis.\n**Hindsight bias:** retrofitting logic onto past actions.\n**Sunk cost fallacy:** the more invested, the harder to abandon.\n**Motivated reasoning:** arguments for what we want to believe feel stronger.\n**Recency bias:** newer information seems more accurate.", cmp("When", ["Planning a study", "Synthesising data", "Explaining users"], "Read group", ["Study design", "Interpretation", "Judging users"]),
   q("After six months on a feature, the team keeps arguing against test results that say users don't need it. Which bias fits best?", ["Sunk cost fallacy", "Serial-position effect", "Clustering bias", "Prestige bias"], "The more time invested, the harder it is to let go.")),
  ("Group 3: Judging users (3) and pile-ups", "**Implicit bias:** automatic associations that affect judgment.\n**Attribution error:** over-crediting personality, under-crediting context.\n**False consensus effect:** believing others think and speak like we do.\n\nBiases stack. Dismissing a finding because “it came from a new user” can combine the power-user fallacy, myside bias and attribution error at once.", None,
   q("“That participant just wasn't very technical.” Which bias is most likely at work?", ["Attribution error", "Recency bias", "Framing effect", "Negativity bias"], "It blames the person and ignores the context: the design."))],
 [q("How is each bias presented in Calabro's guide?", ["Definition, real example, antidote", "Name, severity score, owner", "History, formula, citation", "Symptom, cost, deadline"], "Three parts, so it works as a quick reference."),
  q("What is the main purpose of the guide?", ["Give the team shared vocabulary", "Prove that research is unreliable", "Replace the need for usability tests", "Rank researchers by objectivity"], "Spotting and naming a bias gives you the power to manage it."),
  q("Which bias is in the “study design” group?", ["Serial-position effect", "Confirmation bias", "Attribution error", "Hindsight bias"], "It affects how lists and stimuli are presented, so randomise and rotate."),
  q("You are about to synthesise interview data. Which group should you re-read?", ["Interpretation", "Study design", "Judging users", "None; biases only affect planning"], "Confirmation bias, sunk cost and motivated reasoning show up during synthesis."),
  q("What is “bias bias” in his list?", ["Experts trusting complex explanations over simple ones", "Seeing bias in every finding you dislike", "Blaming users for the team's mistakes", "Preferring recent data to older data"], "It is one of the ten study-design biases.")])
